<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 10px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Saying Please and Thank You – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="font-size: 1.1em; color: #1a5276;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Pre-K (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Meeting My Friends</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Saying Please and Thank You</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Use simple words and gestures to communicate. Interactive: Respond to basic greetings.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can use simple words and gestures for greetings, like using a wave to say "hello" or "goodbye" (e.g., saying "hello" when meeting a friend).
            Can introduce oneself using "My name is [name]." (e.g., when asked to introduce themselves).
            Can respond to basic greetings and polite expressions in simple exchanges, such as "hello," "hi," and "thank you" (e.g., responding with "hi" when greeted or saying "thank you" when given something).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts with a "Hello Song" (e.g., "Hello, hello, how are you?"). Students stand up and participate with TPR gestures (waving, showing "happy" face). The teacher walks around, greets individual students, saying "Hello, [student's name]!" and encourages them to respond with "Hello!" This activates prior knowledge of greetings and the word "hello."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher uses large flashcards or puppets representing a "friend" and a "teacher." The teacher models greetings and introductions: "Hello! My name is Teacher [Teacher's Name]." (waving and pointing to self). Then, with the "friend" puppet, "Hello! My name is Tim." The teacher emphasizes syllable stress in "friends" and the initial /f/ sound. The teacher also models how to wave to say "hello" and "goodbye."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up the "friend" flashcard) "Is this a teacher or a friend?" (Students point or gesture 'friend').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Waving hand) "Am I saying 'hello' or 'goodbye'?" (Students wave back for 'hello' or make a 'bye' gesture for 'goodbye').</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, colorful animated story or a picture book featuring two children meeting, playing, sharing a toy, and then saying goodbye. The story explicitly uses "hello," "My name is [name]," "please," "thank you," and "goodbye." (e.g., "Hello, my name is Mia. What is your name?" "My name is Leo. Nice to meet you!" "Please share the ball." "Thank you!" "Goodbye!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Using polite expressions (saying "please" and "thank you" when interacting with others) and politeness in greetings ("hello," "goodbye," "thank you").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> While watching/listening to the story, the teacher pauses and asks simple questions, prompting short verbal or gestural responses from students.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(After the characters say "hello") "What did they say?" (Elicit "hello" or a wave).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(When one character asks for a toy) "Did she say 'please'?" (Students nod 'yes' or say "yes").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(When a character receives a toy) "What did he say?" (Elicit "thank you").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice Subject + Verb ("I am [name]"), WH-questions ("What is your name?"), Yes/No Questions ("Are you [name]?"), and using vocabulary: "friend," "name," "say," "is," "play," "wave," "meet," "nice," "happy," "please," "hello," "goodbye," "thank you." Focus on initial /f/ in "friend" and /g/ in "goodbye" and correct syllable stress. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Echo the Leader:</b> The teacher says a phrase clearly and slowly, emphasizing target pronunciation, and students echo.
                            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Hello!" (Students: "Hello!")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "My name is [teacher's name]." (Students: "My name is...")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Please!" (Students: "Please!")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Thank you!" (Students: "Thank you!")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Goodbye!" (Students: "Goodbye!")</li>
                            </ul>
                            The teacher uses gestures for each phrase.
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled - Meet and Greet Circle:</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by turning to the student next to them: "Hello! My name is Teacher [Teacher's Name]. What is your name?" The student responds: "My name is [Student's Name]." The teacher then asks the student: "Are you [Student's Name]?" The student says "Yes!" or "Yes, I am." This continues around the circle. The teacher prompts students to wave when they say "hello" and "goodbye."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Sharing Toys:</b> Students work in pairs (or small groups of three). The teacher provides simple, safe toys (e.g., blocks, soft balls). One student holds a toy. The other student asks for it: "Please give me the [toy]!" The first student hands it over and the second student says "Thank you!" Then they switch roles. The teacher circulates, offering prompts and correcting pronunciation if needed, focusing on "please" and "thank you."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play "Playground Fun" in small groups (2-3 students).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students pretend they are on a playground. One student "meets" another "friend."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They practice the following sequence:
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Wave and say "Hello!"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Introduce themselves: "My name is [name]." "What is your name?" (Student responds with their name).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Optionally, they can say "Nice to meet you!" or "Are you happy to play?"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engage in a simple shared activity (e.g., pretend to pass a ball). One student asks "Please give me the ball!" and the other responds "Thank you!" when receiving it.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When they finish playing, they wave and say "Goodbye!" (emphasizing initial /g/ sound).</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, provides encouragement, and offers prompts like "What do you say when you meet a friend?" or "Don't forget 'please'!" or "Say 'thank you'!" as needed. The focus is on natural, albeit simple, interaction and using the target language.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment of speaking skills)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "Playground Fun" role-play for the teacher or the whole class (if time permits, in very small groups). The teacher observes their interaction.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "hello" and a wave for greeting.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Introduces self using "My name is [name]."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to "What is your name?" with "My name is [name]."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "please" when asking for something.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "thank you" when receiving something.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "goodbye" and a wave when leaving.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation is generally intelligible for target words ("hello," "goodbye," "please," "thank you," "name," "friend").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> The teacher asks students to show a thumbs up if they said "hello" and "goodbye" to a friend today. Then, another thumbs up if they said "please" and "thank you."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you use your nice words ('please' and 'thank you') with your friends today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who did you say 'hello' and 'goodbye' to?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to promote speaking (e.g., TPR, pair work, specific activity)? The "Meet and Greet Circle" and "Sharing Toys" activities were particularly effective for encouraging simple, direct speech and polite expressions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was challenging for the students or for me as a teacher? Some students found it difficult to initiate "What is your name?" without direct prompting. Maintaining the correct syllable stress for "goodbye" was also a bit challenging for some.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to support student learning? I need to incorporate more choral repetition of "What is your name?" and provide more opportunities for students to hear and produce the /g/ sound at the beginning of "goodbye" with specific drilling. Pairing stronger students with weaker ones for the role-play could also be beneficial.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing) will build on the spoken greetings and polite expressions by introducing very simple written forms of "hello," "My name is..." and perhaps drawing pictures related to "friends" and saying "thank you."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>